    "story": "The radio is off, the fire is low, and the house has gone down to the rustle of the paper turning. She has already read the headline twice and she is reading it a third time because the third time is when she will believe it — or when she will stop believing it. The photograph on the mantel is a young man in uniform, and she has not looked at it once since she sat down, because looking at it would mean asking the paper a question it cannot answer. The house is warm and the paper is cold in her hands, and the war has come into the room without knocking, the way it has done every morning since he left.",
    "notes": "A war set that never shows the people waiting at home is only half a war set, and this frame is the other half. A woman in a red snood, back to us, reading the D-Day news by a fire that has gone low, a young soldier's photograph on the mantel beside the radio, and the low fire and the colder paper make the waiting itself the subject. It belongs in a lived-in room, not a gallery wall — a kitchen, a hallway, somewhere the news of the day actually gets read — because this frame was made for the places where people sit and wait for word. The headline is what the history books remember. The woman holding the paper is what a collector remembers.",
