    "story": "The crossing took all night, and the night took everything the sea could give them — diesel in the throat, cold in the bones, men sick over the side in the dark. Dry ground was supposed to be the end of it. It isn't. \"Find cover!\" — the first dry order of the day, and the strangest: run from the water that carried you here toward the ground that's trying to kill you. The beach is only the door. The day is on the other side of it.",
    "notes": "If a collector wants the D-Day story in a single frame, this is the frame that tells it. British troops coming off the ramp and onto the sand, packs on, rifles up, a man on the right waving them on, and the water at their backs looks like it is still deciding whether to let them go. There is urgency in every stride here — the scurry, the pointing arm, the sea at their backs — and a wall hangs this frame for the movement in it: the landing itself, caught in the minutes when the beach was still the whole war. The landing is the headline. The man on the right, still pointing, still waving them on, is the reason the frame works.",
