    "story": "An ambush is mostly an argument with your own body. Lie still. Breathe quieter than the grass. A fly walks your ear like it owns the place, and swatting it could kill everybody. Out ahead of them, men are walking through an ordinary morning, not knowing the field has already decided something about them. The grass knows. The grass is keeping the secret — for now, the grass is on their side.",
    "notes": "A collector who walks a gallery slowly will stop at this one twice — once for the grass, and once for the men in it. Three Canadian soldiers lying prone in tall grass on a sandy dune, rifles aimed forward, waiting for orders that have not come yet, and the men sit inside the field's own greens and sands, part of the ground rather than figures on it. It belongs where the room is quiet and the wall gets a long look, because the frame asks the person looking at it to lie still and look carefully, the way the men in it are doing. The grass makes the frame work, and the wait keeps it on the wall.",
