    "story": "It was supposed to live in the sky. A balloon on its tether is a promise — nothing flies low over this beach — and a promise lying in the sand is just a big soft shape full of gas that nobody wants to be the first to test. So nobody shoots at it. Men who will fire at anything armored sit polite under its shadow and wait for somebody senior to decide what you do with a fallen sky. The planes, if they come, will find the whole beach already looking up.",
    "notes": "Every war set has one frame that stops people cold, and this is the war set's. A barrage balloon — a thing that belongs in the sky — drifting low over a beachhead, its shadow falling across the sand where American soldiers lie prone behind a bent hedgehog, rifles out, and everything is lit flat and ordinary, which is what makes the impossible in it so unsettling. It belongs on the biggest wall in the house, because a balloon this big needs room to hang, and the shadow it throws across the beach needs room to fall. The balloon is what you hang. The shadow is what you live with.",
