    "story": "Somebody has to cross it, and the lot fell to him. Walking it would be a kind of dying slow, so he runs — runs the way a man runs when the far side owes him his life back. Every step is a coin spent in the open, where whoever is watching does his arithmetic. His lungs are burning money too. The trees at the far end are not cover yet. They're a promise, and promises out here get kept about half the time.",
    "notes": "Some frames in the war set stand still, and this one does not. The man is caught mid-stride with everything he owns on his back and his rifle across his chest, and the run fills the whole frame. A collector who wants the set to feel like it moves will give this one the wall the eye lands on first, and the warm painterly color keeps the urgency from turning grim — the run stays in the frame, and the frame stays on the wall.",
