    "story": "You hear them before the field ever shows them. A shift in the ground out there, a rhythm that isn't wind — the war arrives by ear long before it arrives by eye, and his ears have already stood up in the hole. Half of him is still down in the dirt where it's safe. The other half has a job. A foxhole teaches a man to be two men at once: the one who survives, and the one who watches. Tonight both of them are listening to the same thing coming.",
    "notes": "The frame to buy when the collection needs a man and not a battle. He is only halfway out of the hole, rifle up, eyes on ground that has not moved yet, and the dirt and the netted helmet and the cloudy sky all do their part without saying a word. A collector hangs it low, at eye level, because the man in it is looking straight out of the frame, and a wall that looks back at you is worth having.",
