    "story": "His hand is a listening post. Under it, a chest either rises or it doesn't, and the medic has learned to give a chest its time — men have come back from quieter than this, and he has been wrong in both directions and paid for both. Everything about the morning says too far gone. The hand says not yet decided. Between the two of them is where a medic lives: in the seconds a man is neither, while the war waits at a respectful distance it doesn't actually feel.",
    "notes": "This is the frame buyers walk past and then come back to. It doesn't show a rescue and it doesn't show a death — it holds the second in between, a medic's hand on a still chest, and refuses to tell you how it ends. That refusal is the brag: most war art resolves; this one trusts the room to stand in the not-knowing with it. Collectors who take it home say the same thing — it is the picture their guests ask about last and remember first.",
