    "story": "Shoulder to shoulder, two trades. His puts rounds out over the concrete as fast as the rifle will take them; the man low behind him practices the opposite trade — everything the rounds do, undone with bandages and calm hands. Nobody shoots at the medic, and everybody shoots near him, so he lives his whole war in the margin of other men's aim. The firing is the loud half of the partnership. It is not the half that gets remembered at reunions.",
    "notes": "A collector stops in the hall for the brass, because the brass is frozen in the air and the air is not. The soldier is down behind the concrete with his rifle over the top, the casing just out of the rifle and already on its way down, and the medic's red-cross helmet is behind the barrier at his side, low, because the frame will not let you forget the medic. A collector hangs it at the height of a man's shoulder, so the rifle is level with the eye and the frame stays level with the room.",
