    "story": "Cold is the enemy that never sleeps and never misses. It takes fingers, toes, engines, tempers — and a column in winter fights two wars on the same road, one against the men ahead and one against the air itself. So a sergeant's laugh is equipment. It goes down the line like rations. Whatever the joke was, it isn't the joke anymore; it's proof, passed man to man in the snow, that the cold hasn't won the only ground that matters.",
    "notes": "Snow pictures in a war set are usually about endurance. This one is about the opposite — a sergeant laughing in the middle of a winter column, and the laugh doing the work a fire would do if they dared light one. That's the uniqueness a collector can claim: it is the set's one winter frame where morale, not misery, is the subject. It hangs well where people gather in the cold months, and it starts more conversations than frames twice its size.",
