    "story": "You are what the sights are for. Not a metaphor — the bead is on you, the cheek is welded, and the only thing between this moment and the next one is a decision a young man is making with his whole body gone still. He has not fired. Whatever you are — enemy, camera, ghost — you have been given the rarest gift the war hands out: a look down the wrong end, and time to be grateful it stopped there.",
    "notes": "The barrel points out of the frame, and the room does not get to look away. The soldier holds his cheek against the stock with his eye down the sights, the sling loose under the rifle and another man crouched below him, and the mottled backdrop keeps the whole frame on the face behind the gun. A collector hangs it at eye level, straight on, so the rifle stays level with whoever walks in.",
