    "story": "Behind is where the safe things live — the boat, the beach as it was, every morning that didn't try to kill him. A man isn't supposed to spend his looks there. But the ground behind has a way of following a soldier: a sound half-heard a field back, a feeling between the shoulders that outlasts the reason for it. So he sits, lets the legs have their minute, and spends one long look making sure the past is staying where he left it. Then the pack goes back to being heavy, and the day resumes.",
    "notes": "Hang it where a visitor stops to look back, and the frame will look back with him. The soldier sits with his pack still on and his legs stretched out in front of him, his head turned over his shoulder at the ground he just crossed, and the canteen and the straps and the hazy ground carry the rest of the frame. A collector gives it the quiet wall, where the turn in the man's neck has room to hold the eye.",
