    "story": "The lean tells the mileage. A fresh man walks upright; a man with a hundred fields in his legs walks like this — angled into the day the way you lean into a wind that never quite stops blowing. Nobody will put this part on a poster. No charge, no flag, just a soldier and the scrub and the next mile being negotiated out of the ground one step at a time. Wars are mostly this. The battles are only where the walking stops.",
    "notes": "No battle in this frame, and no battle needed. The soldier leans into the walk with his rifle across his body and his pack high on his back, the scrub brushing his boots and the brush coming up around him, and the whole frame moves at the pace of a man who has been walking a long time. A collector hangs it where the light runs low, so the lean pulls the eye across the wall and the walk keeps going.",
