    "story": "Firing a rifle is a way of going deaf and blind on purpose. The man working the rifle over the beam has traded the whole field for one sight picture, one strip of ground, one job. So the watching is done by the other man — the quiet half of the weapon. He hasn't fired. His round is attention, and he spends it on everything the shooter has agreed not to see: the flanks, the sky, the treeline that hasn't spoken yet and will. When he speaks, the shooter will believe him. That's the arrangement that keeps them both alive.",
    "notes": "The casings hang in the air and the room hangs on them. The shooter has his rifle up over the beam with the brass still coming out of it, the watching man stands by with his rifle upright, and the steel of the hedgehog runs across the frame in front of them both. A collector hangs it where the eye can travel the beam, so the brass leads the eye and the watching man holds it.",
