    "story": "They can't see it. That's the thing about the flag over this march — the men inside the picture walk under a weight only the rest of us can see, and they carry it anyway, the way they've carried it since the first morning: in letters folded into pockets, in town names spoken like passwords, in the simple agreement that quitting would be reported back to streets that raised them. The flag isn't decoration to a marching man. It's the audience. You walk differently when your whole country's watching, even when the watching is only in your head.",
    "notes": "The stripes run across the whole wall, and the men walk across it with them. The sergeant leads with three chevrons on his sleeve and his rifle in both hands, the men behind him keeping his pace, and the flag laid over the whole scene keeps the march in step. A collector puts it where the flag catches the light first, and the march follows.",
