    "id": "i-ZwPshf2",
    "title": "The Firing Line",
    "description": "American soldiers fire from a line, the man in front leaning into his rifle with a red patch on the side of his helmet and his webbing across his chest, other soldiers aiming beside him through the smoke. The painterly color treatment holds the frame in warm, muted tones — the khaki uniforms, the worn wood of the rifles, and the hazy smoke all carrying the same soft light.\n\nPart of a broader body of painterly fine art photography, this World War II frame holds the firing line: the rifles up, the eyes down the sights, and the smoke hanging behind them where the last shot went.\n\nThe line stays where it is, and the rifles stay up, and the smoke stays behind them.\n\n© Wayne Heim",
    "alt": "A painterly color photograph of American World War II soldiers firing from a line, the man in front leaning into his rifle with a red patch on the side of his helmet, other soldiers aiming beside him through the smoke. Painterly fine art photography, World War II, Facing History.",
